    <article id="fullArticle">
        <h1 class="title">How to use Team Feeds and Team Boards</h1>
    
        <a href="javascript:window.print()" class="printArticle" title="Print this article" aria-label="Print this Article"><i class="icon-print"></i></a>
        <div class="video video-vimeo video-responsive" style="aspect-ratio:16 / 9;">
 <iframe width="100%" height="100%" frameborder="0" allowfullscreen allow="autoplay; fullscreen; picture-in-picture" src="https://player.vimeo.com/video/932520381?title=0&amp;byline=0&amp;portrait=0" title="Vimeo video player"></iframe>
</div>
<br>
<p>When you’re using Feedly as part of an enterprise team, it helps to understand how your&nbsp;<strong>team feeds</strong>,&nbsp;<strong>boards</strong>, and&nbsp;<strong>personal feeds</strong>&nbsp;work together, and why organizing them well makes your workflow smoother.</p>
<h1><strong>Team Feeds</strong></h1>
<h2><strong>What they are:</strong></h2>
<p>Team Feeds automatically update as new content is published. They are organized into Folders, which you can expand or collapse. Team Feeds include:</p>
<ul>
 <ul>
  <li><a href="https://feedly.com/i/aiFeeds"><strong>AI Feeds</strong>:</a>&nbsp;Powered by AI Models and filters to surface high-signal intel.</li>
  <li><a href="https://feedly.com/i/discover"><strong>RSS Feeds</strong></a>: Custom sources you or your team have added.</li>
 </ul>
</ul>
<h2><strong>Best practice:</strong></h2>
<ul>
 <li>
  <p>Keep&nbsp;<strong>AI feeds</strong>&nbsp;and&nbsp;<strong>RSS feeds</strong>&nbsp;in&nbsp;<strong>separate folders</strong>. For example, you might prefix AI feed folders with “AI” to make them easy to spot.</p>
  <p>➜ Why? AI Feeds automatically filter out noise and surface relevant intel. RSS feeds pull in&nbsp;<strong>everything,</strong>&nbsp;so mixing them can muddy the signal.</p>
 </li>
</ul>
<h2><strong>How to use:</strong></h2>
<ul>
 <ul>
  <li>Click a&nbsp;<strong>folder</strong>&nbsp;to see all articles from feeds inside it at once.</li>
  <li>Or drill down into a specific AI feed if you want to focus on that topic only.</li>
 </ul>
</ul>
<h1><strong>Team Boards</strong></h1>
<h2><strong>What they are:</strong></h2>
<p>Boards are where you&nbsp;<strong>manually save</strong>&nbsp;and&nbsp;<strong>organize</strong>&nbsp;articles that matter most. They’re perfect for:</p>
<ul>
 <ul>
  <li>Highlighting priority intel for the team.</li>
  <li>Building curated content for a newsletter or briefing.</li>
  <li>Adding&nbsp;<strong>analyst notes</strong>&nbsp;or context for others.</li>
 </ul>
</ul>
<h2><strong>How to use:</strong></h2>
<ul>
 <ul>
  <li>Save an article from any feed to a board.</li>
  <li>Add comments or notes for your teammates.</li>
  <li>Tag people, assign follow-ups, or even share directly to Slack or email — right from Feedly.</li>
 </ul>
</ul>
<h1><strong>Personal Feeds &amp; Boards</strong></h1>
<h2><strong>What they are:</strong></h2>
<ul>
 <li>Personal feeds and boards are&nbsp;<strong>private,</strong>&nbsp;only you can see them.</li>
</ul>
<h2><strong>When to use them:</strong></h2>
<ul>
 <ul>
  <li>Researching sensitive topics that shouldn’t be shared with the full team yet.</li>
  <li>Working on a confidential request from leadership.</li>
  <li>Tracking personal interests or side projects (yes, you can track hobby topics too!).</li>
 </ul>
</ul>
    </article>
    
